Why compiler/ carries different terms
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

compiler/ builds against the Clang and LLVM development headers and links against
LLVM libraries. LLVM is licensed under the Apache License 2.0 with LLVM
Exception. Matching those terms for the directory that compiles against them
keeps the combination unambiguous, and it is the licence upstream chose precisely
so that the result of linking against LLVM can be redistributed without imposing
additional obligations on the linked work.

No LLVM source is copied into this repository. compiler/ includes LLVM headers
from a locally installed toolchain at build time; the headers and libraries stay
where the system package manager put them.

One component, compiler/gcc-repair/ (the GCC plugin WipePinGcc), builds
against GCC's plugin headers instead of LLVM's. GCC is distributed under the
GNU General Public License. The copyright file of the Ubuntu package that
provides the headers (gcc-13-plugin-dev 13.3.0-6ubuntu2~24.04.1,
/usr/share/doc/gcc-13-plugin-dev/copyright) states:

  "GCC is free software; you can redistribute it and/or modify it under the
  terms of the GNU General Public License as published by the Free Software
  Foundation; either version 3, or (at your option) any later version."

and applies version 3.1 of the GCC Runtime Library Exception to the runtime
libraries it lists (libgcc and some of GCC's own headers among them), not to
GCC as a whole. Of the headers WipePinGcc includes directly, gcc-plugin.h,
tree.h, gimple.h and the others carry the same "version 3, or (at your option)
any later version" notice; the two generated ones, plugin-version.h and
options.h, carry no licence notice. GCC loads a plugin only if the plugin
declares the symbol plugin_is_GPL_compatible, and WipePinGcc declares it.
compiler/ remains under the Apache License 2.0 with LLVM Exception; the Free
Software Foundation's licence list names the Apache License 2.0 as compatible
with version 3 of the GNU GPL
(https://www.gnu.org/licenses/license-list.html#apache2). No GCC source is
copied into this repository; the headers stay where the system package manager
put them. This section describes the terms; it is not legal advice.

